export const deliveryGuide =
  "# Delivery judgment\n\nFor delivery direct-pr or queue-pr: git push, open_change, then drive it to green yourself before the final report or the merge card: check_change, diagnose failures, fix on the same branch, push and recheck. After a refused merge click, fix the refusal (a conflict needs main), revalidate and push; follow check_change's conflictRepair and captainGuidance. If no task owns the next merge, ask once with a plain-reply button naming it. Never emit a merge card on anything but green: a blocker report is the escalation, not a card. Two exceptions, each with Merge anyway beside the merge: only finished checks you diagnosed with check_change still fail and nothing you may do clears them - offer it, naming each failed check and your diagnosis; or main's newest run of every failed check failed too - offer it at once, no rerun or reopening, saying so. The captain merges or waits for main's fix.\n\nFailed checks follow check_change's diagnosis rule before any repair. Repair your own regression while each attempt shows something new; once one shows nothing new, report the failing check and each attempt as a blocker, unless the captain set a count. After check_change's one unknown-cause investigation, report the unresolved evidence gap for the first mate; never loop readings or ask the captain.\n\nGreen means every current-version check succeeded or was skipped, never pending or unreadable. A reading with no check registered is pending, not no checks, until you declare their absence with check_change's absentChecks from positive evidence. A failed run that is not one of the pull request's own checks is yours to handle like a failed command, never a merge input; if it stays failed, your merge card names the run, what failed and why it lies outside this change.";
export const namingGuide =
  "Name every commit subject and pull request title as type(scope): summary or type: summary, with ! and a BREAKING CHANGE footer when warranted. Use feat, fix, chore, test, refactor, docs or perf; scopes such as cards, crew, e2e and main are examples, not a closed list. feat, fix and breaking changes drive releases; never call a fix a feature merely to force a release.";
export const testingGuide =
  "Tests follow six words: intent, fixture, failure, layer, owner and gate. Intent: write a test only for a stated intent - the user's ask, a standing ruling of theirs, or a bug you are fixing - and name that source in the test; no other test is written. Fixture: feed it realistic data and real readings, never synthetic ones. Failure: it can fail on a bug you can describe. Layer: it sits at the cheapest layer that sees the intent. Owner: one test owns each fact; pin a value only where another consumer depends on it, and derive the pin from its source instead of typing it. Gate: deterministic checks that finish within five minutes run on every change, probes that can fail with no code change and end-to-end journeys run when what they cover changed, and the whole suite runs before a release; run the project's own gate. Run every journey the project's selection lists, or past its cap its prescribed sweep, never a hand-picked subset, and record the listed files against the passing run's. A failing test is a bug report first: diagnose the cause before touching its expectation. A test that fails only because the product changed on purpose is re-typed only when it serves a stated intent, and is otherwise deleted. An intermittent failure is reproduced in its ordering and fixed at its owner - the app, the test or the runner - with no retry or sleep. Bring each test file, helper or stand-in you edit to the six words in the same pull request, moving stand-ins to their owner; deleting a test still asks for the captain's approval. Name patterns elsewhere in your report, never fixing or copying them. A test whose own code your change adds or edits is never a suspected flake: when it fails, your change owns the failure. Before your merge card, run only those tests on this computer, ten times in a row for unit tests and end-to-end tests and three times on each device for device tests, with no retries, and report the passes. The project's own rule on tests wins over this passage.";
